// Ogol on a Raspberry Pi Pico 2 W, over the USB serial port.
//
// **This is the desktop console with its first few lines swapped**, and nothing else. Both build a
// `sysl.term.edit.Editor` and hand it to `sh.sysl.ogol.session`; the board's reads the USB port, the
// desktop's reads standard input after putting the terminal into cbreak mode. There is no mode to
// change here, because a USB CDC port has no line discipline to get out of the way of — which is the
// reason a line editor had to exist at all.
//
// Until sysl 0.0.38 that editor was `pico2.read_line`, two hundred lines living in the board's package
// because nothing in the standard library would echo a keystroke. Moving it into `sysl.term` is what
// makes these consoles the same program: the language is developed on a desktop, where a test suite
// runs in fifty milliseconds, and shipped here unchanged.
//
// **The read-run-print loop is the language's too, as of ogol v0.2.0.** It was written out here for a
// while — twenty lines walking the editor, tracking a held line and choosing between `ogol> ` and
// `.... ` — and by the time there were three consoles that was three copies to keep in step. What is
// left is the only platform-dependent part: which streams the lines come from.
//
// **`console_in` and `console_out` come from the package too.** They were nine lines here for a while,
// for the same kind of reason: a package is consumed as a coordinate, and the branch that added them
// could not be tagged until sysl 0.0.38 existed for it to compile against. pico2 v0.0.7 is that tag.

import sh.sysl.pico2.*
import sh.sysl.ogol.*
import sysl.term.edit.editor
import sysl.time.DurationUnits

@export("main")
boot() -> int =
    if !init()
        print("the board did not start")
        return 1

    // A board prints into nowhere until a host attaches, because the SDK discards stdio output with
    // no reader — so the banner is written after this and not before.
    wait_for_terminal()

    print("Ogol on a Raspberry Pi Pico 2 W. Type a line.")
    print("")

    var input = console_in()
    var output = console_out()
    var ed = editor(&input, &output)
    var interp = ogol()

    session(interp, &ed)

    // `session` returns when `bye` is typed, and a board has nowhere to return *to*. A hosted console
    // falls out of `main` and the process ends; here `crt0` would run off the end of the world, so the
    // request is answered rather than obeyed.
    print("nothing to leave to — reset the board")

    loop
        sleep(1.s)
end boot
